Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Re-read the actual current HEAD before editing. Most of Batch 7's audit
sections were already correctly implemented (see report below) — this
commit contains only the genuine gaps found.

BACKEND SECURITY/RELIABILITY FIXES

1. tsconfig.json: module/moduleResolution changed from CommonJS/node to
   Node16/node16 — the correct modern pairing for a Node.js CommonJS
   backend, replacing the deprecated node10-style resolution. Verified
   with zero source changes required: npm run build, npm test (all
   passing), npm run dev, and the compiled dist/server.js all work
   identically.

2. src/routes/emails.ts: added a shared emailId route-param validator
   (router.param) rejecting anything that isn't [A-Za-z0-9-]{1,100}
   with a clean 400 INVALID_EMAIL_ID, applied to every :emailId route
   at once. Filesystem paths were already traversal-safe via
   emailStore.ts's existing safeId() character stripping — this adds
   proper input validation on top, so a malformed ID fails cleanly
   instead of being silently mangled into an unrelated lookup that
   404s, and keeps raw malformed input out of error messages.

3. src/services/emailStore.ts: getEmailRecord()/readSummary() now
   catch a JSON.parse failure on a corrupted/truncated stored record
   and convert it to a controlled 500 RECORD_UNREADABLE ApiError,
   instead of letting the raw SyntaxError bubble up and get mapped to
   a misleading 400 BAD_REQUEST by the generic error handler.

4. src/app.ts:
   - Hardened the generic `Error` branch: previously echoed
     err.message verbatim to the client for ANY unclassified Error —
     a latent internal-detail leak vector (e.g. a future uncaught
     parser error). Now logs server-side and returns a generic safe
     message; the one legitimate caller that relied on the old
     behavior (upload.ts's .eml-only file filter) was converted to
     throw the app's own ApiError instead, so it's unaffected and
     reaches the client through the same controlled path as every
     other validation error.
   - Oversized uploads now return 413 Payload Too Large (was 400) —
     matches the spec's explicit requirement; other multer errors
     (wrong field, too many files) stay 400.
   - Added app.disable("x-powered-by") and two zero-dependency safe
     headers (X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, X-Frame-Options: DENY).

5. src/utils/upload.ts: default MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES raised from 5MB to
   ~25MB per spec (real .eml files with attachments commonly exceed
   5MB); fileFilter now throws Errors.invalidFileType() instead of a
   plain Error (see #4).

6. src/utils/apiError.ts: added invalidEmailId(), invalidFileType(),
   recordUnreadable() error constructors, following the existing
   Errors.* convention.

7. src/analyzers/reportBuilder.ts: the "Received Relay Chain" report
   section only exposed a receivedHopCount — added the actual per-hop
   receivedChain array (hop/fromHostname/fromIp/byHostname/
   timestampIso), reusing headerAnalysis's already-computed data
   (OBSERVED provenance, never re-derived), so the frontend report can
   render real relay-chain detail instead of just a count.

CONFIRMED ALREADY CORRECT (audited, no changes needed):
  - List-query param validation (pagination/sort/filter/status/
    classification) — already comprehensive with proper error codes.
  - Filename safety (assertSafeFilename) — already traversal-proof.
  - No exec/spawn/eval anywhere in the codebase.
  - No SSRF risk — nothing fetches attacker-controlled URLs extracted
    from emails; GeoIP/DNS only ever hit their own configured provider
    endpoints (checked geoipClient.ts, dnsClient.ts, urlAnalyzer.ts,
    domainAnalyzer.ts, iocExtractor.ts directly).
  - GeoIP/DNS timeout and failure handling already tested.
  - ML cache behavior (a failed model load stays failed until restart)
    is correct per spec ("configuration changes require restart as
    expected"), not a bug — left unchanged.
  - No secrets logged anywhere (verified across llmClient.ts,
    aiAssessment.ts, and the existing [ai-debug] diagnostics).

FRONTEND — REAL DATA INTEGRATION (Sections 19-24)

8. FRONTEND/src/api/api.ts: added getEmailReport() (GET
   /api/v1/emails/:emailId/report) with a full ApiForensicReport type
   matching the backend's ForensicReportContent exactly, and
   getRelatedEmails() (GET /api/v1/emails/:emailId/related) with an
   ApiRelatedEmails type.

9. FRONTEND/src/pages/ReportsPage.tsx: the "full report" view
   previously fabricated ALL of the following client-side, from the
   lightweight ScannedEmail shape, and said so in its own footer
   ("generated from mock analysis data in a frontend prototype"):
     - mockSha256() — a fake hash function
     - "Assigned Analyst: M. Chen" — a fabricated name
     - confidence derived as threatScore*0.9+8 — explicitly forbidden
       by spec Section 14 ("do not calculate frontend confidence from
       threat score")
     - related-email count computed from the frontend's own mock
       dataset
     - the entire "Related Campaigns" section — every field hardcoded
       to null
     - "Recommended Actions" sourced from an unused mock
       reportSections field, never Batch 5C's real data
   Replaced with a real fetch to GET /api/v1/emails/:emailId/report
   whenever an email is selected, and rewrote every report section to
   render the actual backend fields: real SHA-256/timestamps, real
   risk.score/level/classification/confidence/evidenceCoverage plus
   the category-score breakdown, real header anomalies and the actual
   per-hop received chain, real IOC counts, real ML
   (model/version/classification/probability), real AI
   (attackType/summary/aiContentScore/status — shown as AI UNAVAILABLE
   when no provider is configured, never fabricated), real Batch 5B
   campaign/correlation data, real Batch 5C recommended actions, and
   the real backend limitations array. The single-click preview
   (renderReportsPreview, using the already-correct lightweight
   ScannedEmail shape) was left unchanged — only the fabricated
   full-report content was replaced. Selection/navigation architecture
   (useActiveCase, InvestigationShell, InvestigationWorkspace) is
   untouched.

10. FRONTEND/src/pages/InfrastructurePage.tsx: added a real "Likely
    Related Campaign" card using actual Batch 5B data (getRelatedEmails),
    fetched alongside the existing detail fetch so a correlation-fetch
    failure never blocks the rest of the infrastructure view. The
    existing infrastructure relationship graph visualization (built
    from IP intelligence, not fabricated, just not sourced from the
    dedicated Batch 5A /graph endpoint) was left as-is — see
    limitations below.

TESTS

- tests/security.test.ts (new, 11 tests): traversal-style/path-
  separator/null-byte emailId rejection across multiple routes sharing
  the router.param validator; well-formed-but-nonexistent emailId
  still 404s correctly; corrupted stored JSON returns a controlled 500
  without leaking the raw parser error or any filesystem path;
  non-.eml upload rejected through the ApiError path; X-Powered-By
  absent; safe headers present; no response body ever contains a stack
  trace.
- tests/scan-oversized.test.ts: updated to assert 413 (was 400) per
  the corrected status code.
- tests/reportBuilder.test.ts (+2 tests): the report's received chain
  includes real per-hop data, and is an empty array (not fabricated)
  when header analysis is unavailable.

RESULTS

- npm test: 259/259 passing (13 new/updated, 246 baseline unchanged).
- npm run build: clean (backend and frontend).
- npx tsc --noEmit: clean on the backend; only the same pre-existing,
  unrelated ForensicsPage.tsx errors remain on the frontend (not
  touched, not introduced here — auth status typing, flagged in an
  earlier unrelated fix).
- End-to-end smoke test performed live: scan -> detail (ML AVAILABLE,
  real probability, 2 recommendations) -> graph (real evidence-backed
  nodes/edges) -> related -> report (real receivedChain, real SHA-256)
  -> a deliberately malformed .eml scanned without crashing (201, not
  500).

REMAINING LIMITATIONS (not addressed in this patch, scoped out given
time — flagged for a follow-up rather than rushed):
  - InfrastructurePage's relationship-graph VISUALIZATION still builds
    its nodes/edges from IP intelligence client-side rather than
    fetching Batch 5A's dedicated /graph endpoint directly. Not
    fabricated data, just not the canonical source — lower priority
    than the campaign-correlation gap (which was fully missing) since
    this was already a reasonable, honest simplification.
  - OverviewPage/CasesPage/ScannerPage were not re-audited in this
    pass for residual mock-data usage beyond what earlier fixes in
    this conversation already addressed.
  - No live Gemini network test was possible from this sandbox (no
    egress to generativelanguage.googleapis.com) — AI pipeline
    correctness for a real configured key/model was not verified live
    in this batch (was verified in an earlier session per the user's
    own confirmation that Gemini is now working).
  - Demo dataset composition (Section 32) was not audited — no
    existing demo-seed-data mechanism was found in the current
    codebase to verify against.
